In **Tailwind CSS v4**, configuration has moved from a JavaScript file (`tailwind.config.js`) into your main CSS file using a CSS-first approach[1][2]. 

By default, the `dark:` variant relies on the user's operating system preference (`prefers-color-scheme`)[3][1]. To implement a **manual dark mode toggle via a CSS class** (e.g., `<html class="dark">`), you can override Tailwind's default behavior using the `@custom-variant` directive[3][1].

### 1. Configure your main CSS file
Add the `@custom-variant` rule right below your Tailwind import[1]:

```css
/* app.css or index.css */
@import "tailwindcss";

/* Override the default 'dark' variant to use a selector strategy */
@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *));
```

* **`&:where(.dark, .dark * [, ...])`**: This tells Tailwind that whenever an element (or any of its ancestors up to the root) has the `.dark` class, any utility prefixed with `dark:` should apply[4][1].

---

### 2. Use it in your HTML
You can now use `dark:` classes normally across your markup[1][5]:

```html
<div class="bg-white text-black dark:bg-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
  <p>Hello World</p>
</div>
```

---

### 3. Toggle it via JavaScript
To toggle between light and dark modes, add or remove the `dark` class from a parent element (usually the root `<html>` tag)[6][5]:

```javascript
// Switch to dark mode
document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');

// Switch to light mode
document.documentElement.classList.remove('dark');

// Toggle dynamically
document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark');
```

